<header><p class="enc-kicker">Event</p><h1 class="enc-title">Dionysia</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as City Dionysia, Great Dionysia</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Dionysia were Athenian festivals of the god <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dionysus">Dionysus</a>, the greatest being the City Dionysia held each spring. From the late sixth century BCE it included contests in tragedy, later also comedy and satyr plays, for which the works of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aeschylus">Aeschylus</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sophocles">Sophocles</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/euripides">Euripides</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aristophanes">Aristophanes</a> were written. It was also an occasion for displaying the tribute of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/athens">Athens</a>' allies.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/dionysia.jpg" alt="Dionysia" width="320" height="237" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown artist · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Delos_Museum_Mosaik_Dionysos_05.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p>The Dionysia consisted of two related festivals, the Rural Dionysia and the City Dionysia, held in different parts of the year. The rural festival was originally held at Eleutherae in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/attica">Attica</a> to celebrate the cultivation of vines, and took place in the winter month of Poseideon. Its central event was a procession in which phalloi were carried, accompanied by basket-carriers and bearers of loaves, water jars and goatskin bags of wine. Contests of dancing and singing followed, and in larger towns such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/piraeus">Piraeus</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eleusis">Eleusis</a> there may have been dramatic performances. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aristophanes">Aristophanes</a> parodied the rural festival in The Acharnians, and his description is the earliest surviving account.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Course of events</h2><p>The City Dionysia was established under the tyranny of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peisistratus">Peisistratus</a> in the 6th century BCE. It was held probably from the 10th to the 16th of the month Elaphebolion, in March and April, and was run by the archon, who chose two assistants and ten curators. By tradition it began when Eleutherae joined <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/attica">Attica</a> and brought a statue of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dionysus">Dionysus</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/athens">Athens</a>, where it was at first rejected; Dionysus then sent a plague, which ended when the Athenians accepted the cult. On the first day citizens, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/metic">metics</a> and delegates from colonies carried the statue to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/theatre">Theatre</a> of Dionysus.</p><p>The next day, at the proagon, playwrights announced the titles of their plays and judges were chosen by lot. Tradition holds that Thespis gave the first tragedy at the festival in 534 BCE. In the 5th century BCE three playwrights each presented three tragedies and a satyr play on successive days, and from 487 or 486 BCE comic poets also competed, each with one play. Most surviving Greek tragedies, including those of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aeschylus">Aeschylus</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sophocles">Sophocles</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/euripides">Euripides</a>, were first performed in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/theatre">Theatre</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dionysus">Dionysus</a>. Winners received a wreath of ivy.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>Tragic output continued through most of the 4th century BCE, and at least one older play was revived each year. New plays were written until the 2nd century BCE, when new works of comedy and tragedy appear to have ceased. Drama continued, but prizes went to wealthy producers and prominent actors rather than to the long-dead playwrights. Knowledge of the winners comes from damaged inscriptions, the Fasti, the Didascaliae and the Victors Lists. Modern followers of Hellenism celebrate the Dionysia as a holiday.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Dionysia” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Religious life centred on Athena Polias, honoured every four years in the Great Panathenaia, and on festivals such as the <b>City Dionysia</b>, where tragedies and comedies were staged, and the Eleusinian Mysteries of Demeter and Kore.</blockquote><a href="/empire/athens">Athens · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>In Athens the Panathenaia honoured Athena with a procession, shown on the frieze of the Parthenon, which brought a new robe for her ancient statue, with athletic and musical contests; the <b>City Dionysia</b> brought new tragedies and comedies before the citizens.</blockquote><a href="/religion/ancient-greek-religion">Ancient Greek religion · Community &amp; Society</a></li><li><blockquote>…public water where aristocrats had kept private wells, began the vast Temple of Olympian Zeus, enlarged the Panathenaea and founded or enlarged the <b>City Dionysia</b>, where the first tragedies were staged around 534 BCE; tradition also credits them with the first attempt at a fixed text of Homer.</blockquote><a href="/government/tyranny">Tyranny · Strengths</a></li></ul></section>
